      title: "Rewilding: Letting Nature Take Back Control",
      passage: "For centuries, conservation has largely meant protecting nature as it is — fencing off reserves, managing habitats carefully and saving individual species from extinction. In recent years, however, a bolder approach has gained popularity. Known as rewilding, it aims to restore ecosystems to a more natural state and then, as far as possible, step back and allow natural processes to do the rest.\n\nOne of the most famous examples took place in Yellowstone National Park in the United States. Wolves had been hunted to extinction there by the 1920s, and without predators, the number of elk grew dramatically. The elk ate young trees along the rivers, causing riverbanks to erode and many other species to decline. When wolves were reintroduced in 1995, the effects were remarkable. The elk changed their behaviour, avoiding open valleys where they were vulnerable, and trees began to recover. Birds returned, beavers built dams that created new wetlands, and the whole ecosystem began to change. Scientists describe this chain reaction as a 'trophic cascade'. Some researchers now believe the early accounts exaggerated the wolves' role, but the case remains a powerful symbol.\n\nIn Europe, rewilding projects have taken different forms. In England, the Knepp estate, once an intensive farm that was losing money, was allowed to return to nature in the early 2000s. Instead of crops, free-roaming herds of cattle, ponies, pigs and deer were introduced to mimic the behaviour of ancient wild animals. Within two decades, the estate had become one of the most important wildlife sites in the country, home to rare birds, butterflies and, in 2020, the first wild beavers in the area for centuries.\n\nRewilding is not without controversy. Farmers often worry that reintroduced predators such as wolves and lynx will attack their livestock, and some rural communities feel that decisions are being made by city-based conservationists who do not understand their way of life. There are also concerns about food production: if land is taken out of farming, food may need to be imported from elsewhere, potentially causing environmental damage in other parts of the world.\n\nSupporters respond that rewilding does not have to mean abandoning farming everywhere, and that it can create new jobs in tourism and land management. Its greatest lesson may be a humbling one: that nature, given the chance, is often better at repairing itself than we are at managing it.",
