      title: "Can We Slow Down Ageing?",
      passage: "Throughout history, humans have dreamed of living longer. Ancient myths told of fountains of youth and magical fruits that granted eternal life. Today, the search continues — but in laboratories rather than legends, and funded by some of the richest people on the planet. Several technology billionaires have invested huge sums in companies dedicated to understanding and slowing the ageing process. But how realistic are their ambitions?\n\nFirst, some perspective. Average life expectancy has already increased dramatically over the past two centuries, from around thirty years globally in 1800 to over seventy today. However, most of this improvement came from reducing deaths in childhood and early adulthood, through better sanitation, vaccines and medicine, rather than from extending the maximum human lifespan. The oldest person on record, a French woman named Jeanne Calment, died in 1997 at the age of 122, and that record has not been broken since.\n\nScientists increasingly view ageing not as an inevitable process of wear and tear, but as a set of biological processes that might be influenced. These include damage to DNA, the shortening of protective structures at the ends of chromosomes called telomeres, and the build-up of so-called 'zombie cells' — cells that have stopped dividing but refuse to die, releasing substances that harm the tissue around them. In experiments on mice, removing these cells has extended healthy lifespan and delayed age-related diseases.\n\nOther research has focused on diet. Studies on a variety of animals, from worms to monkeys, have found that significantly reducing calorie intake can extend lifespan. Whether this applies to humans is not yet clear, and few people would be willing to eat so little for their whole lives. Scientists are therefore searching for drugs that might produce similar effects without the need for extreme dieting.\n\nMany experts emphasise that the most important goal is not simply to live longer but to extend 'healthspan' — the number of years we live in good health. Living to 110 would be far less attractive if the final thirty years were spent in poor health.\n\nThere are also ethical questions. If treatments to slow ageing become available, will only the wealthy be able to afford them? And how would society cope with a population in which people routinely lived well beyond a hundred? For now, the most reliable advice remains rather old-fashioned: don't smoke, stay active, eat well, sleep enough and maintain close relationships.",
